  A SINGLE MACHINE DEFAULT, so a fresh install runs without configuring a
  cluster. It is not maintained by hand. The moment conf/nodes.conf exists,
  bin/bt-cluster policy writes this file from it, and bin/oodt rewrites it
  at every start.

  It was maintained by hand once, and it went stale. Conditions were given a
  node id of their own, so that long running translations cannot exhaust the
  pool the gates need, and this file was not updated. An upgrade unpacked it
  over the generated policy, nothing served the "conditions" queue any more,
  and every gated task became unschedulable: a run that crawls, extracts,
  and then sits there. bin/bt-cluster preflight reports it as

      manager: queue [conditions] is asked for but no node serves it

  Three node ids on one machine, because the Resource Manager tracks load
  per node id rather than per queue. A queue that shares an id shares its
  capacity: the join once sat queued behind 131 translate jobs on a full
  node after every translation had already succeeded.
-->
<cas:nodeMap xmlns:cas="http://oodt.jpl.nasa.gov/1.0/resource">
  <node id="local">
    <queues>
      <queue name="translate"/>
    </queues>
  </node>
  <node id="local-managers">
    <queues>
      <queue name="managers"/>
    </queues>
  </node>
  <node id="local-conditions">
    <queues>
      <queue name="conditions"/>
    </queues>
  </node>
</cas:nodeMap>
